<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="rubber biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Natural rubber is an important raw material in an enormous number of products. The backbone of natural rubber comprises a (&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;)-1,4-polyisoprene with two or three &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-isoprene units at the &amp;omega;-terminus, formed by the sequential condensation of |FRAME: DELTA3-ISOPENTENYL-PP| (isopentenyl pyrophosphate, IPP) units in &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; configuration onto a short &lt;i&gt;all-trans&lt;/i&gt; prenyl diphosphate. It is very similar in structure to |FRAME: DOLICHOL dolichols|, but the chain length is much longer (C&gt;1500). Natural rubber is produced from latex - a material that makes up the cytoplasmic content of the latecifers or latex vessels of |FRAME: TAX-3981| (the Brazilian rubber tree) |CITS: [17545224]|. Although over 2000 plant species capable of producing rubber have been identified, only a few synthesize the high molecular weight rubber that is required for the production of high quality rubber products, and natural rubber is currently produced commercially only from |FRAME: TAX-3981|. Potential alternative sources are the Russian rubber-producing dandelion (|FRAME: TAX-333970|), which was cultivated in the former Soviet Union during World War II to satisfy the demand for natural rubber |CITS: [22504822]|, and guayule (|FRAME: TAX-35935|), a xerophytic perennial shrub that produces high quality natural rubber and is endemic to the Chihuahuan desert |CITS: [29116401][33513965]|. Rubber biosynthesis is carried out by |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.20|. The enzyme is localized at the surface of cytosolic vesicles known as rubber particles, and biosynthesis is initiated through the binding of an allylic pyrophosphate primer, such as |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP| |CITS: [Tanaka01][14622254][18799172]|. The source of IPP are the isoprenoid pathways (see |FRAME: PWY-922| and |FRAME: NONMEVIPP-PWY|). Although conventionally the cytoplasmic |FRAME: PWY-922 &quot;mevalonate pathway&quot;| was considered to be the source of IPP for the biosynthesis of rubber, some studies suggest that the plastidic |FRAME: NONMEVIPP-PWY &quot;methylerythritol phosphate pathway&quot;| may provide an alternative means of generating IPP for rubber synthesis |CITS: [15010614]|. In 2003 two cDNAs encoding &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-prenyltransferases, sharing 88% of the predicted amino acid sequence of their products, were isolated from |FRAME: TAX-3981| and named |FRAME: G-54973| and |FRAME: G-54976| |CITS: [14622254]|. Expression of |FRAME: G-54973| is restricted to the latex, while that of |FRAME: G-54976| has been observed in various tissues |CITS: [25017153]|. Upon addition of fresh rubber particles from |FRAME: TAX-3981|, purified recombinant |FRAME: G-54976| was shown to catalyze |FRAME: DELTA3-ISOPENTENYL-PP| incorporation and form a rubber polymer of 3,00015,000 isoprene units |CITS: [14622254]|. The activity of |FRAME: G-54973| was demonstrated only in 2016, when it was discovered that activity requires formation of a complex with two other proteins - a rubber elongation factor (|FRAME: G-54974|), and the HRT1-REF bridging protein (|FRAME: G-54975|). |FRAME: G-54975| appears to act as a bridge, bringing the other two components together. Activity of the complex was demonstrated by using a cell-free translation-coupled system with detergent-washed rubber particles. This system enabled &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis of natural rubber &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;, providing the first direct evidence that |FRAME: G-54973| participates in rubber biosynthesis |CITS: [27790974]|. &lt;b&gt;Rubber biosynthesis by |FRAME: TAX-35935| (guayule)&lt;/b&gt; The desire to reduce the environmental cost of rubber production, the need for pesticides for growing clonal |FRAME: TAX-3981| plantations in non-native environments, and increasing labor costs for &lt;i&gt;Hevea&lt;/i&gt; rubber production motivate efforts to use alternative crops for rubber production. Guayule has major advantages over &lt;i&gt;Hevea&lt;/i&gt; since it can be cultivated broadly across temperate and arid climates and it produces high molecular weight natural rubber suitable for use in the manufacture of tires. In addition, harvesting of biomass and rubber extraction are easily mechanized. The one significant disadvantage of guayule is the fact that rubber is produced only under cold temperature in the winter months. Four guayule genes - three &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-prenyltransferases (CPs) and one CPT-binding protein that can form complexes with any of the CPTs, have been identified in guayule, and one of the CPTs was shown to be predominantly expressed in stem and induced by cold-stress, suggesting involvement in rubber biosynthesis |CITS: [30858856]|. Transcriptome analysis of rubber biosynthesis in guayule has identified several new enzymes potentially involved in the biosynthesis of rubber as well as transcription factors specifically expressed in rubber-producing tissues |CITS: [30755179]|. However, as of 2022 the pathway for rubber biosynthesis in guayule is not fully understood.</Description>
