      <Xref identifier="53405" dataSource="ChEBI" />
      <Graphics centerX="879.8489990234375" centerY="1007.4013671875" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>Natural rubber is an important raw material in an enormous number of products. The backbone of natural rubber comprises a (&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;)-1,4-polyisoprene with two or three &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-isoprene units at the &amp;omega;-terminus, formed by the sequential condensation of |FRAME: DELTA3-ISOPENTENYL-PP| (IPP) units in &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; configuration onto a short &lt;i&gt;all-trans&lt;/i&gt; prenyl diphosphate. It is very similar in structure to |FRAME: DOLICHOL dolichols|, but the chain length is much longer (C&gt;1500). Natural rubber is produced from latex - a material that makes up the cytoplasmic content of the latecifers or latex vessels of |FRAME: TAX-3981| (the Brazilian rubber tree) |CITS: [17545224]|. Although over 2000 plant species capable of producing rubber have been identified, only a few synthesize the high molecular weight rubber that is required for the production of high quality rubber products, and natural rubber is currently produced commercially only from |FRAME: TAX-3981|. A potential alternative source is the Russian rubber-producing dandelion (|FRAME: TAX-333970|), which was cultivated in the former Soviet Union during World War II to satisfy the demand for natural rubber |CITS: [22504822]|. Rubber biosynthesis is carried out by |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.20|. The enzyme is localized at the surface of cytosolic vesicles known as rubber particles, and biosynthesis is initiated through the binding of an allylic pyrophosphate primer, such as |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP| |CITS: [Tanaka01][14622254][18799172]|. The R in the structure stands for an unspecified number of (&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;)-connected isoprene units.</Comment>
